format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

lesson what-stops-what
title: What stops what
minutes: 6
xp: 50
teaches: u3-penetration
teaches: u3-gamma-shielding
relies_on: u2-recognise-radiation
relies_on: u3-ionizing-meaning

description:
    Alpha, beta and gamma travel very different distances through things. You will find out what stops each one, and why gamma rays need something thick and dense.

cite ref-epa-radiation-basics

supports:
    Alpha particles cannot penetrate the outer layer of skin; beta particles are stopped by a thin layer of aluminum; several inches of lead or a few feet of concrete may be required to stop gamma rays.

cite ref-openstax-chem-21-6

supports:
    A sheet or two of paper, or the top layer of skin cells, usually stops alpha particles; beta particles are stopped by a thin layer of metal; gamma radiation is very penetrating.

cite ref-epa-protecting-yourself

supports:
    Barriers of lead, concrete or water protect from gamma rays and X-rays; dentists place a lead apron on patients having X-rays of their teeth.

card c01
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l2-predict-paper
scored: 0

card c02
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Alpha: heavy, so it stops fast
figure: alpha-paper
figure_at: 2

body:
    An alpha particle is heavy. A heavy particle uses up its energy over a very short distance. So a sheet or two of paper, or the top layer of your skin, usually stops it.

card c03
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l2-choice-alpha
connected: true

card c04
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Beta: small and fast
figure: beta-metal
figure_at: 2

body:
    A beta particle is small and fast. It goes farther through air than an alpha particle, and it passes through paper. A thin layer of metal, such as aluminum, stops it.

card c05
kind: item
type: item
item: u3-l2-choice-beta
connected: true

card c06
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Gamma: very penetrating

body:
    Gamma rays are pure energy, and they are very penetrating. They pass through skin and clothing. Paper and a thin layer of metal will not stop them.

card c07
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: What it takes to stop gamma
figure: gamma-thick
figure_at: 1

body:
    It takes several inches of a dense material like lead, or a few feet of concrete, to stop gamma rays. Thick and dense: that is what makes a good shield against them.

